      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl text-ink mb-2">7. Buying jars online — the plain-jar wholesale path</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-ink/60 mb-6">The simplest procurement model for pilot scale: buy plain unbranded jars + caps from a wholesaler online, label them yourself. No factory negotiation. No custom mold tooling. No 5,000-piece printing MOQ. This is how most small Indian D2C food brands operate for the first 6–12 months.</p>

      <h3 class="font-serif text-xl text-ink mt-4 mb-3">Why this works — plain IS the default</h3>
      <p class="sup-body">
        Indian stock glass jars come <strong>without printed branding</strong>:
      </p>
      <ul class="sup-bullets">
        <li>Clean transparent glass body — no logos, no markings on customer-facing surfaces</li>
        <li>A tiny <strong>mold seam / parting line</strong> on the side (production artifact, not branding) — should be on the side, NOT crossing the rim</li>
        <li>A small <strong>manufacturer mold code</strong> on the bottom (e.g., "HNG", "BG/450") — invisible because the jar sits upright on the shelf</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="sup-body" style="margin-top: 12px;">
        Stock caps come in plain colors (gold, silver, black, white) <strong>with no logo printed on them</strong>. Possible tiny mold mark on the inner skirt — invisible once on the jar.
      </p>
      <p class="sup-body" style="margin-top: 12px;">
        So "plain jar + plain cap" = <strong>no visible branding on the customer-facing surfaces</strong>. This is the standard offering. Branding (foil-stamped MGS on cap top, custom mold logo on jar) is the upcharge, not the default.
      </p>

